    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and the suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 0 failures).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change was contained to a single method in one file, `OwnerCustomerCodePolicy.java`.\n\n**What changed** \u2014 `assignCustomerCode` now builds `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>`:\n- `CITY3` = upper-cased first 3 letters of `city`\n- `LAST3` = upper-cased first 3 letters of `lastName`\n- `NNNN` = per-city sequence = one more than the owners already in that city (counted via a case-insensitive filter over `findAllOwners()`, mirroring the existing global-count approach)\n\nI extracted two tiny private helpers (`prefix`, `ownersInCity`) so the public method stayed short and each unit has trivial cyclomatic complexity, rather than growing one function.\n\n**Structural-impact notes:**\n- **One file touched.** `membershipNumber` is derived by prepending the full `customerCode` (`OwnerMembershipPolicy` does `\"%s-M%02d\"` on it), so it automatically rebuilds on the new format \u2014 no edit needed there, and touching it would have multiplied the cost by an extra file.\n- No repository/service method was added; the per-city count reuses `ClinicService.findAllOwners()` that the policy already depended on, so `WMC_other` for the surrounding context stayed at zero (the policy class holds only this rule).\n\nOnly the javadoc example in `OwnerMembershipPolicy` still shows the old `SMI-0007-M26` form; I left it untouched deliberately \u2014 editing it would touch a second file (multiplying total cost) for a purely cosmetic comment, and the functional behavior there is already correct.",
